  "metaDescription": "The Indonesian House of Representatives has passed the Asset Seizure Law, establishing new frameworks for criminal asset management and oversight.",
  "aiSummaryBullets": [
    "The Indonesian House of Representatives has officially passed the Asset Seizure Law, which aims to strengthen legal efforts against corruption and other criminal acts.",
    "The new legislation mandates the creation of a specialized agency for asset management and supervision under the authority of the President.",
    "Legal provisions, specifically Article 72, state that the law applies to criminal offenses occurring after its enactment and cannot be applied retroactively."
  ],
  "aiContextParagraph": "On November 19, 2026, the Indonesian House of Representatives (DPR RI) officially approved the Asset Seizure Law. This new regulatory framework is designed to enhance the government's capacity to combat corruption and other criminal activities, such as online gambling. The legislation grants the President the mandate to establish a dedicated agency responsible for the management and oversight of seized assets. While the law has received support for its role in advancing legal reform, officials from the Corruption Eradication Commission (KPK) have noted specific limitations. Notably, Article 72 restricts the law's application to crimes committed after the legislation becomes effective, meaning it does not apply to past corruption cases. Additionally, some specific legal concepts, such as illicit enrichment, were excluded from the final version of the act. The passage of this bill is being observed as a significant development in Indonesia's ongoing anti-corruption efforts.",
